play":0.07745}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2022,"team":"DAL","distance":0.3994,"shared":24,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2024,"team":"TEN","distance":0.553,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"NYG","distance":0.6052,"shared":24,"play_caller":"Mike Kafka"},{"season":2022,"team":"PIT","distance":0.6371,"shared":24,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"DET","distance":0.6439,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Ben Johnson"}],"defense":[{"season":2019,"team":"CIN","distance":0.5935,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2018,"team":"SEA","distance":0.5965,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6817,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2018,"team":"LA","distance":0.7102,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"WAS","distance":0.7552,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Browns went 7-10 in 2022 and were outscored 381 to 361. The offense carried the team, ranking 10th in EPA per play while the defense finished 22nd in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the 3rd fewest backs in the backfield and the 3rd highest eight man box rate in the league.","offense":"The Browns offense was well above average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd fewest backs in the backfield in the league. Jacoby Brissett ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Amari Cooper, who drew 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Browns defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 12th and 29th. Myles Garrett was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Taven Bryan, who had 3 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 3rd highest eight man box rate in the league.","special":"The Browns were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 64% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 75% of field goals, which was one of the worst in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their secondary was among the youngest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job was split, with Jacoby Brissett and Deshaun Watson both getting meaningful snaps. Amari Cooper was the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 1,160 receiving yards. Nick Chubb handled most of the work in the backfield with 68% of the carries. On defense, Myles Garrett led the team with 16 sacks and Grant Delpit picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Kevin Stefanski called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2022 Cowboys under Kellen Moore and the 2024 Titans. Their defense looked most like the 2019 Bengals under Lou Anarumo and the 2018 Seahawks.","trends":"The offense went from 18th in EPA per play in 2021 to 10th in 2022, a big step forward. The defense moved from 14th in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 22nd in 2022, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was fourth down go rate, which went from 17% to 64%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}